/-
# EDOL GRAMMAR INFINITENESS is NP-complete

Source:
M. R. Garey and D. S. Johnson, *Computers and Intractability: A Guide to the
Theory of NP-Completeness*, W. H. Freeman (1979), problem [AL16] ETOL GRAMMAR
NON-EMPTINESS, p. 270, comment ("NP-complete if G contains just one table and exactly one
production for each symbol (G is an "EDOL grammar"). The same results hold for the question
of whether G generates an infinite language.");
N. D. Jones and S. Skyum, "Complexity of some problems concerning L systems", Proc. 4th
International Colloquium on Automata, Languages and Programming (ICALP 1977), Lecture Notes
in Computer Science 52, Springer (1977), 301-308, doi:10.1007/3-540-08342-1_23;
journal version: Mathematical Systems Theory 13 (1979), 29-43, doi:10.1007/BF01744286.
-/
module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import CSLibExt.Computability.Complexity.Defs
public import MathlibExt.Computability.BinaryNatEncoding
public import MathlibExt.Computability.Complexity
public import Mathlib.Basic.Finite.Defs
public import WantedExt.Complexity.NPComplete.EDOLGrammarNonEmptinessWanted

@[expose] public section

namespace MetaMathlibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.EDOLGrammarInfiniteness

/-!
# EDOL GRAMMAR INFINITENESS is NP-complete

Given an EDOL grammar `G = (N, Σ, {δ}, S)` (an ETOL grammar with just one table and exactly
one production for each symbol), does `G` generate an infinite language?

This is the infiniteness question of Garey and Johnson's comment on [AL16], for the grammars
of EDOL GRAMMAR NON-EMPTINESS
(`WantedExt.Complexity.NPComplete.EDOLGrammarNonEmptinessWanted`). The instance, its
`WellFormed` (`S` is a nonterminal, productions over `N ∪ Σ`, one table with exactly one
production for each symbol) and the derivation step `Step` (every symbol of the current string
is rewritten by a production of the chosen table) are reused unchanged. The language generated
by `G` is the set of strings of terminals derivable from `S` in zero or more steps; the
question asks whether this set is infinite (`Set.Infinite`).

Encoding: the instance is decoded from a bitstring with the repository's
canonical `MetaMathlibExt.BitstringEncoding`. Undecodable bitstrings, and
bitstrings decoding to malformed instances, are no-instances.

NP-completeness is stated in the same form as the proved results in
`CSLibExt.Computability.Complexity.Karp`: membership in
`MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP`, plus a polynomial-time many-one
reduction from every language in `NP`
(`Cslib.CookLevin.PolyManyOneRed`).
-/

open EDOLGrammarNonEmptiness (EDOLGrammarNonEmptinessInstance LSymbol Step WellFormed)

/-- The language generated by the grammar: the strings of terminals derivable from `S` in zero
or more steps. -/
def generatedLanguage : EDOLGrammarNonEmptinessInstance → Set (List LSymbol)
  | (_, _, ts, S) =>
    {v | (∀ y ∈ v, y.1 = true) ∧ Relation.ReflTransGen (Step ts) [(false, S)] v}

/-- The language generated by the grammar is infinite. -/
def EDOLGrammarInfinitenessFeasible (i : EDOLGrammarNonEmptinessInstance) : Prop :=
  (generatedLanguage i).Infinite

open Classical in
/-- EDOL GRAMMAR INFINITENESS as a decision problem over bitstrings. -/
noncomputable def edolGrammarInfinitenessDec :
    MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.DecisionProblem := fun x =>
  match MetaMathlibExt.BitstringEncoding.bitDecode
      (α := EDOLGrammarNonEmptinessInstance) x with
  | some i => decide (WellFormed i ∧ EDOLGrammarInfinitenessFeasible i)
  | none => false

/-- **EDOL GRAMMAR INFINITENESS is NP-complete.** Source: Garey and Johnson (1979), [AL16],
comment; Jones and Skyum (1977, 1979). -/
public theorem_wanted edolGrammarInfiniteness_npComplete :
    edolGrammarInfinitenessDec ∈ MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP ∧
      ∀ L : MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.DecisionProblem,
        L ∈ MetaMathlibExt.ComplexityTheory.NP →
          Cslib.CookLevin.PolyManyOneRed L edolGrammarInfinitenessDec

end MetaMathlibExt.Complexity.NPComplete.EDOLGrammarInfiniteness
